Saved: `docs/content-analysis/perspective-reviews/Nate-Bargatze/2026-08-25_020002/fan.md`

**Verdict:** `revise` — trust strained, value high, delight clear_hit. **4 blockers, 8 concerns.**

**Strongest hit (FAN-H3):** the Emmys bit read as an indirect enforcement machine — *"He has designed a machine in which other people's self-indulgence visibly takes money away from children, and it never once points at him. The arithmetic does the enforcing."* Every fan watched that bit and argued about it; nobody framed it that way. That plus "Nobody let him in the building. So he started buying buildings." is why this scores useful-to-high rather than corrective.

**Highest-priority blocker (FAN-R1):** *"In 2026 he added a 62-date arena tour"* is wrong three ways — the Big Dumb Eyes tour began April 2025, ran 129 venues, and **ended nine days before this draft was dated**, when Bargatze was handed a Guinness World Record onstage in Toronto for most tickets ever sold on a stand-up tour (2,045,040, beating Jeff Dunham). I fetched BroadwayWorld directly to confirm. The man whose entire brand is being average now holds an all-time world record, and the article doesn't know — that's both a factual error and the loss of the best sentence available to the piece.

The other three blockers: the specials count is five not four (the draft names the fifth itself 68 lines later); the cold open's *"Nineteen years and 365 days"* is contradicted by the draft's own "started stand-up in 2002" plus an April 2023 Bridgestone date; and *"his most famous childhood story"* — the assistant giving away the trick — is a story fans don't recognize, whose only corroboration is an AI book-summary site. I fetched the AP review of *Big Dumb Eyes* to check independently; it doesn't mention the anecdote at all. That claim carries the persona title, a TL;DR bullet, and the closing image.
